1. Java Objects (including arrays), custom objects are passed by reference which means that when you pass an object to
a method, the method gets a reference (or pointer) to that object, not a copy of the object itself.
So, when you modify the object's state (it's properties or values) inside the method, those changes will be reflected
in the original object from the calling code because both the method and the calling code are referencing
the same object in memory.
Strings and Wrapper classes are immutable so even if they are object as passed by reference , the change is not
reflected in the calling part

Arrays and Objects are passed by reference in java
This means that changes made to the array or object in the method will affect the original
array or object in the calling code.
Primitive Data Types (like int, double etc) are passed by value
When you pass a primitive to a method the method gets a copy of the value, so changes inside the method
do not affect the original value in the calling code.

public class Example {
    public static void modifyArray(int[] array) {
        array[0] = 100; // Modifying the array element
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = {1, 2, 3};
        modifyArray(numbers); // Passing array to the method
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(numbers)); // Output: [100, 2, 3]
    }
}


public class Example {
    public static void modifyValue(int value) {
        value = 100; // Modifying the value (won't affect the original)
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int num = 5;
        modifyValue(num); // Passing primitive to the method
        System.out.println(num); // Output: 5 (no change)
    }
}


//shallow and deep copy in js
In js the terms shallow copy and deep copy refer to how objects and arrays are copied. The difference lies in whether
nested objects or arrays are copied as well or if they are simply referenced

Shallow Copy:
A shallow copy means that only the first level of the object or array is copied. Nested objects or arrays are still
references to the original, so change made to nested structure in the copy will affect the original as well.

Deep Copy:
A deep copy creates a new copy of the object or array, including all nested objects or arrays. So, changes to nested
structures in the copy will affect not the original object

Using spread operator (...) will make shallow copy
If you need to perform a deep copy, you would need to use other methods like JSON.parse(JSON.stringify())
or manually deep-copy the objects.

Using JSON.parse() and JSON.stringify() (Quick and easy for plain objects and arrays)
This method serializes the object into a JSON string and then deserializes it back into a new object or array.
It performs a deep copy, but it comes with some limitations, such as not handling functions, undefined, RegExp,
Date, Map, Set, or circular references.

const original = {
  name: 'Alice',
  age: 25,
  address: { city: 'Wonderland', zip: 12345 },
  hobbies: ['reading', 'coding']
};

const deepCopy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));

deepCopy.address.city = 'Dreamland';
deepCopy.hobbies[0] = 'traveling';

console.log(original.address.city); // "Wonderland" (original object is unchanged)
console.log(original.hobbies[0]); // "reading" (original object is unchanged)

console.log(deepCopy.address.city); // "Dreamland"
console.log(deepCopy.hobbies[0]); // "traveling"

